  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 142 PM CDT Sun Sep 20 2026\n\nPrecipitation activity largely wound down by mid-morning, leaving \nthe area predominantly blanketed in broken to overcast skies near \nand north of the cold front and more sunshine ahead / south of the \nfront. Do not expect much, if any, additional precipitation activity \nthrough the early-mid afternoon for much of the area for a few \nreasons... noted capping ahead of the front and limited remaining \ntime (before frontal passage) to mix it out, shallow frontal passage \nyielding sizable inversion post-frontal, and limited synoptic scale \nsupport (even weak subsidence) to produce any elevated activity. \nHave noted some brief and transient drizzle may be possible with hi-\nres sounding suggesting up to 1.5km stratus depth as stratus \ncontinues to drop in from the north. Generally much ado about \nnothing. Otherwise, the cooler temperatures are likely the main \nstory line for most, and more on that in a minute. \n\nThere remains a chance for a few renewed convective showers and \nthunderstorms late afternoon into the evening, but with the main \ncold front now south of the area and approaching the I-44 corridor, \nthe chances are waning quickly, locally. The HRRR has been adamant \nof this southern solution since yesterday among its extended runs, \nwhile synoptic solutions have tended to be further north. Other CAM \nguidance, has tended to favor south as well, such as majority of the \n12z HREF members. Have trended PoPs drier than NBM thusly.\nRegardless, in the unlikely event a storm clips the southern CWA, \nthe primary risk would be for lightning.\n\nLooking bigger picture, the pattern is a bit messy and yields some \nlingering light precipitation chances through Monday. This as a \nweak/unorganized low-level/850mb low lifts NE across the area \ntonight and a mid-level/500mb open wave to weakly closed low drifts \neastward across Nebraska/Iowa. Neither will produce anything of \nsignificance, but will linger at least lower-end (<35-40 percent) \nPoPs over N to NE Missouri through Monday. Then by Tuesday and \nthrough the middle of the week deterministic guidance suggests a \nmessy attempt at a Rex Block over the Great Lakes region. Late week \nsees ridging move back into the central CONUS, but may be hindered \nby the evolution of the aforementioned attempted Rex Block. At the \nsurface, all of this too tends to linger a sizable surface high \naround the Great Lakes. This is notable as said surface high is the \nsource of current cold frontal passage and air mass. So, the main \ntakeaway/result from all of this for the work week ahead is much \nwelcome cooler, fall-like, temperatures. Expect highs in the 60s to \n70s and lows predominantly in the 50s. Precipitation chances do try \nto move back in late work week, but will be dependent on how just \nhow blocked up the eastern CONUS pattern will be.",
